@startuml class-core
title Core types and the three stage contracts — by Rust kind

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam ArrowColor #444444

' the kind is carried by the BOX colour, so it reads at a glance
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor<<struct>> #EAF4FB
  BorderColor<<struct>>     #2E86C1
  BackgroundColor<<enum>>   #EAF7EF
  BorderColor<<enum>>       #229954
  BackgroundColor<<trait>>  #FDF0E3
  BorderColor<<trait>>      #CA6F1E
  BackgroundColor<<fn>>     #FEF9E7
  BorderColor<<fn>>         #B7950B
  BackgroundColor<<impl>>   #F4ECF7
  BorderColor<<impl>>       #7D3C98
}

legend top
  **Every box says what KIND of Rust item it is.**

  |<#EAF4FB> **S  struct** | data. ""+"" public field, ""-"" private and reached by an accessor |
  |<#EAF7EF> **E  enum** | a closed set |
  |<#FDF0E3> **T  trait** | a contract an impl must satisfy; associated types listed as ""type X"" |
  |<#FEF9E7> **F  fn** | free function, no receiver — traversal written once so no author writes it |
  |<#F4ECF7> **B  impl** | an impl the FRAMEWORK provides, so an author inherits the behaviour |

  Arrows are Rust relations, not UML ones — each is labelled with the
  construct it stands for (""field"", ""supertrait"", ""type Output ="", ""impl"").
endlegend

' ===========================================================================
package "data — what flows between stages" as DATA <<Rectangle>> {

  class "Extraction<T>" as Extraction <<(S,#AED6F1) struct>> {
    +value: Option<T>
    +reasons: Vec<Reason>
    --
    +value(T) -> Self
    +failed(Reason) -> Self
    +with_reason(Reason) -> Self
    +is_failure(&self) -> bool
    +absorb<U>(&mut self, Extraction<U>) -> Option<U>
  }

  class "Extracted<T, I>" as Extracted <<(S,#AED6F1) struct>> {
    -extraction: Extraction<T>
    -source: I
    --
    +new(Extraction<T>, I) -> Self
    +source(&self) -> &I
    +value(&self) -> Option<&T>
    +reasons(&self) -> &[Reason]
    +extraction(&self) -> &Extraction<T>
    +into_extraction(self) -> Extraction<T>
    ..where I: ToTokens..
    +to_error(&self, msg) -> syn::Error
  }

  class Reason <<(S,#AED6F1) struct>> {
    +kind: ReasonKind
    -span: Option<Span>
    --
    +new(ReasonKind) -> Self
    +at(ReasonKind, &impl Spanned) -> Self
    +span(&self) -> Option<Span>
    +message(&self) -> String
    +to_error(&self, &impl ToTokens, msg) -> syn::Error
  }

  enum ReasonKind <<(E,#A9DFBF) enum>> {
    WrongShape
    UnknownKey
    Missing
    Duplicate
    Ambiguous
    Custom(String)
    --
    +message(&self) -> String
  }
}

' ===========================================================================
package "contracts — what each stage must provide" as CONTRACTS <<Rectangle>> {

  interface "Validate<'ast, I: Visitable<'ast>>" as Validate <<(T,#F5CBA7) trait>> {
    +type ValidityError
    +type Valid
    --
    +validate(I) -> Result<Valid, ValidityError>
  }

  interface "Extractor<'ast, I: Visitable<'ast>>" as Extractor <<(T,#F5CBA7) trait>> {
    +type Output
    --
    +extract_from(I) -> Output
  }

  interface Processor <<(T,#F5CBA7) trait>> {
    +type Input
    +type Output
    --
    +process(Input) -> Extraction<Output>
  }

  interface Generator <<(T,#F5CBA7) trait>> {
    +type Input
    --
    +generate(Input) -> TokenStream
  }

  interface Diagnose <<(T,#F5CBA7) trait>> {
    +diagnose(&self, out: &mut Vec<syn::Error>)
  }

  interface "Visitable<'ast>" as Visitable <<(T,#F5CBA7) trait>> {
    +accept<V: Visit<'ast>>(self, &mut V)
  }
}

' ===========================================================================
package "free functions — traversal written once" as FNS <<Rectangle>> {

  class "extractor::{ extract, extract_each, extract_maybe }" as FromHelpers <<(F,#F9E79F) fn>> {
    extract<T, I>(I) -> T::Output
    extract_each<T, I, N>(N) -> Vec<T::Output>
    extract_maybe<T, I>(Option<I>) -> Option<T::Output>
  }

  class "processor::{ process_each, process_maybe }" as ProcHelpers <<(F,#F9E79F) fn>> {
    process_each<P, N>(N) -> Vec<Extraction<P::Output>>
    process_maybe<P>(Option<P::Input>) -> Option<Extraction<P::Output>>
  }

  class "render::{ render, combined }" as RenderFns <<(F,#F9E79F) fn>> {
    render(&impl Diagnose) -> Vec<syn::Error>
    combined(Vec<syn::Error>) -> Option<syn::Error>
  }

  class "generator::{ emit, emit_errors }" as EmitFns <<(F,#F9E79F) fn>> {
    emit(stub, &[Reason], &impl ToTokens, msg) -> TokenStream
    emit_errors(stub, Vec<syn::Error>) -> TokenStream
  }
}

' ===========================================================================
package "framework impls — what an author does NOT write" as IMPLS <<Rectangle>> {

  class "impl Diagnose for Extracted<T, I>\nwhere T: Diagnose, I: ToTokens" as ImplExtracted <<(B,#D7BDE2) impl>> {
    renders THIS node's own reasons
    then descends into value()
  }

  class "impl<T: Diagnose> Diagnose for Vec<T>" as ImplVec <<(B,#D7BDE2) impl>> {
    every element, in order
  }

  class "impl<T: Diagnose> Diagnose for Option<T>" as ImplOption <<(B,#D7BDE2) impl>> {
    the Some arm, or nothing
  }
}

' ===========================================================================
' relations, each labelled with the Rust construct it stands for
Extracted *-- Extraction : field
Extraction o-- "0..*" Reason : field
Reason *-- ReasonKind : field

Extractor --|> Validate : supertrait
Extractor ..> Extracted : "type Output ="
Extractor ..> Visitable : "bound on I"
Validate ..> Visitable : "bound on I"

Extractor ..> Processor : "Output becomes\nProcessor::Input"
Processor ..> Generator : "Output becomes\nGenerator::Input"

FromHelpers ..> Extractor : calls
ProcHelpers ..> Processor : calls
RenderFns ..> Diagnose : calls
EmitFns ..> RenderFns : consumes\nVec<syn::Error>

ImplExtracted ..|> Diagnose : impl
ImplVec ..|> Diagnose : impl
ImplOption ..|> Diagnose : impl
ImplExtracted ..> Extracted : for

' ===========================================================================
note right of Extraction
  **A struct, NOT a two-state.**
  A node can extract fine AND carry a complaint:
  an unknown key is the PARENT failing to consume
  its input while the value stays good. A Result
  would force a choice and discard something true.

  Value and reasons are ORTHOGONAL axes, so they
  get orthogonal fields. This is what lets
  ID(no-result) hold downstream — no ""?"", no early
  return, so no sibling is ever dropped.

  No lifetime: see Reason.
end note

note right of Extracted
  **Private fields, public accessors** — unlike
  Extraction, whose fields are public. The
  asymmetry is deliberate: Extraction is a value
  a stage BUILDS, Extracted is a wrapper a stage
  RECEIVES and must not tear apart.

  The extractor's OUTPUT and the processor's
  INPUT, handed over WHOLE.

  The source lives here, not behind a
  ""T: Sourced"" bound, because a FAILED extraction
  has no T to ask — the case a diagnostic needs
  most. (#extracted/source-when-absent)

  No lifetime parameter: ""I"" is always a borrowed
  node or a Copy wrapper, so it carries 'ast.
end note

note left of Reason
  Stores a **Span**; Extracted stores a **node**.
  Deliberate asymmetry:

  · a node is multi-token, so it needs
    ""new_spanned"" for its full range
  · a reason points at ONE token, so it needs no
    ""Span::join"" and is exact

  ""None"" means nothing finer than the node it sits
  on, and the walk falls back. ""to_error"" takes
  that node as an ARGUMENT — a reason cannot
  render itself, because position belongs to
  where it SITS. (#reason/span-not-node)

  ""Span"" is Copy and owns nothing, which is why
  neither Reason nor Extraction has a lifetime.
  (#no-owned-nodes)
end note

note bottom of ReasonKind
  **Closed reasons, open rendering.**
  Closed so the framework can interpret what it
  caught; ""Custom"" so an exotic grammar is never
  blocked.

  ""message()"" is the OPEN half: wording is
  produced LATE, at render time, never baked in
  when the reason is recorded — so an author's
  own diagnostics can replace it without the
  framework losing the span or the tree position.

  Three variants are ALSO obtainable free from
  rustc by re-emitting keys as a struct literal
  — see the GENERATION lane of 02-pipeline.
  ""WrongShape"" and ""Ambiguous"" stay ours.
end note

note bottom of Validate
  Separate from Extractor because it has a REAL
  hand-written case: StructExtraction narrows
  ""&DeriveInput"" to ""&DataStruct"", and the syntax
  stage narrows to ""(&Attribute, SyntaxHelper)"".

  Not vestigial — it was UNDEFINED, which is why
  every impl used to be ""Ok(input)"".
  (#pipeline/validity-scope, #derive/three-not-one)
end note

note bottom of Visitable
  Implemented for the REFERENCE types
  (""&'ast syn::Attribute""), so ""self"" is already
  the ""&'ast T"" every ""visit_*"" wants.

  Implementing it for the OWNED type made the
  receiver unconstructible for value-passed
  wrappers. (#visitable/by-value)
end note

note bottom of Processor
  **Optional stage.** ""#[derive(Processor)]"" emits
  the identity, so downstream sees ONE shape and
  never an either-or.

  Takes the extractor's ""Output"" WHOLE — the
  pipeline never unwraps between stages, because
  unwrapping strips the source off the value that
  most needs it.

  The bottom-up cascade is a HELPER, not a
  signature: pre-processing children would need
  the parent's Input to be its own extraction
  with every child field swapped for that child's
  Output — a type-level struct transformation
  Rust cannot express. So the order is a
  DISCIPLINE the author follows, not something
  the type system enforces.
end note

note bottom of FromHelpers
  What ""#[from(..)]"" lowers to. Which one a field
  uses is read off its TYPE, never off the
  attribute (#from/arity-from-type).

  Call sites must NAME T: ""T::Output"" is an
  associated type and so not injective, so it
  cannot be inferred from the field.
  (#from/names-its-target)
end note

note bottom of RenderFns
  ONE depth-first walk, every reason in the tree.

  Returns a COLLECTION, not a TokenStream, so the
  entry point can interleave these errors with
  the processor's before anything is emitted —
  a stream would commit too early.

  Does NOT sort by span. VERIFIED impossible AND
  unnecessary: ""Span::start"" is gated and returns
  line 0 inside a real macro, and depth-first
  over a source-ordered syn tree already IS
  source order. (#render/traversal-is-source-order)

  Still owed: (#render/dedupe-and-cap).
end note

note top of IMPLS
  **This is why an author never writes traversal.**

  ""Diagnose"" asks a value only WHERE ITS CHILDREN
  ARE. It never renders its own reasons — the
  ""Extracted"" wrapping it does, because that is
  the only thing holding the node those reasons
  span against. (#render/who-renders)

  The three impls below cover the container shapes
  a field can have, so a grammar type's whole
  obligation is one line per child field — which
  is exactly what ""#[derive(Extractor)]"" emits.
  (#derive/diagnose-rides-along)

  A LEAF is the one case the derive cannot state,
  and omitting it is a COMPILE ERROR, never a
  silent gap.
end note

note bottom of EmitFns
  ""emit_errors"" is the whole-tree form and the one
  an entry point should use; ""emit"" is the
  single-node convenience.

  Either way the stub goes out FIRST. Without it
  the missing impl cascades into "does not
  implement" at every use site and buries the real
  diagnostic. (#generator/stub-is-not-empty)

  TODO(#typed-output/generate): both should return
  a TYPED syn item. We BUILD this content and know
  its shape, so untyped means nothing checks it
  until rustc re-parses — and precise spans are
  impossible. Carrier vs product: ""Unresolved"" and
  ""ListBody"" stay raw, deliberately.
end note

@enduml
